WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:09.250 --> 00:12.480
CHAIR YELLEN.

00:18.390 --> 00:20.890
Good afternoon.

00:20.890 --> 00:24.440
The Federal Open Market
Committee concluded its last

00:24.440 --> 00:28.100
meeting of the year
earlier today.

00:28.100 --> 00:31.330
As indicated in our
policy statement,

00:31.330 --> 00:36.120
the FOMC "reaffirmed its
view that the current 0

00:36.120 --> 00:38.600
to one quarter percent
target range

00:38.600 --> 00:42.930
for the federal funds
rate remains appropriate."

00:42.930 --> 00:46.010
The Committee also updated
its forward guidance

00:46.010 --> 00:48.040
for the federal funds rate,

00:48.040 --> 00:52.690
indicating that "the Committee
judges that it can be patient

00:52.690 --> 00:57.680
in beginning to normalize the
stance of monetary policy."

00:57.680 --> 01:00.910
This new language does
not represent a change

01:00.910 --> 01:04.970
in our policy intentions
and is fully consistent

01:04.970 --> 01:09.090
with our previous
guidance, which stated

01:09.090 --> 01:11.250
that it likely will
be appropriate

01:11.250 --> 01:15.090
to maintain the current target
range for the federal funds rate

01:15.090 --> 01:19.160
for a considerable
time after the end

01:19.160 --> 01:22.260
of our asset purchase program.

01:22.260 --> 01:26.150
But with that program
having ended in October,

01:26.150 --> 01:27.920
and the economy continuing

01:27.920 --> 01:32.360
to make progress toward our
objectives, the Committee judged

01:32.360 --> 01:33.810
that some modification

01:33.810 --> 01:36.870
to our guidance is
appropriate at this time.

01:36.870 --> 01:40.560
I will have more to say
about our policy decisions

01:40.560 --> 01:44.700
in a moment, but first let
me review recent economic

01:44.700 --> 01:46.720
developments and the outlook.

01:46.720 --> 01:52.220
In the labor market, progress
continues toward the FOMC's

01:52.220 --> 01:55.740
objective of maximum employment.

01:55.740 --> 01:59.090
The pace of job growth
has been strong recently,

01:59.090 --> 02:03.610
with job gains averaging
nearly 280,000 per month

02:03.610 --> 02:08.330
over the past 3 months;
over the past 12 months,

02:08.330 --> 02:13.190
job gains averaged
nearly 230,000 per month.

02:13.190 --> 02:17.700
The unemployment rate was
5.8 percent in November,

02:17.700 --> 02:20.790
three-tenths lower than the
latest reading available

02:20.790 --> 02:24.870
at the time of the
September FOMC meeting.

02:24.870 --> 02:28.840
Broader measures of labor market
utilization have shown similar

02:28.840 --> 02:33.060
improvement, and the labor
force participation rate has

02:33.060 --> 02:35.260
leveled out.

02:35.260 --> 02:39.200
As noted in the FOMC
statement, "underutilization

02:39.200 --> 02:43.250
of labor resources
continues to diminish."

02:43.250 --> 02:46.560
Even so, there is room
for further improvement,

02:46.560 --> 02:51.240
with too many people who want
jobs being unable to find them,

02:51.240 --> 02:53.210
too many who are
working part time

02:53.210 --> 02:57.410
but would prefer full-time work,
and too many who have given

02:57.410 --> 03:00.140
up searching for a job
but would likely do

03:00.140 --> 03:03.270
so if the labor market
were stronger.

03:03.270 --> 03:07.630
The Committee continues to see
sufficient underlying strength

03:07.630 --> 03:11.230
in the economy to support
ongoing improvement

03:11.230 --> 03:13.660
in the labor market.

03:13.660 --> 03:18.930
Real GDP looks to have increased
robustly in the third quarter,

03:18.930 --> 03:23.370
reflecting solid consumption
and investment spending.

03:23.370 --> 03:28.020
Smoothing through the quarterly
ups and downs earlier this year,

03:28.020 --> 03:32.130
real GDP expanded around
2 and a half percent

03:32.130 --> 03:35.930
over the four quarters
ending in the third quarter,

03:35.930 --> 03:37.950
and the available
indicators suggest

03:37.950 --> 03:41.010
that economic growth is
running at roughly that pace

03:41.010 --> 03:43.320
in the current quarter.

03:43.320 --> 03:46.380
The Committee continues
to expect a moderate pace

03:46.380 --> 03:49.340
of growth going forward.

03:49.340 --> 03:51.140
Inflation has continued

03:51.140 --> 03:55.110
to run below the Committee's
2 percent objective,

03:55.110 --> 03:59.760
and the recent sizable declines
in oil prices will likely hold

03:59.760 --> 04:02.830
down overall inflation
in the near term.

04:02.830 --> 04:06.890
But as the effects of
these oil price declines

04:06.890 --> 04:10.550
and other transitory
factors dissipate

04:10.550 --> 04:14.600
and as resource utilization
continues to rise,

04:14.600 --> 04:16.430
the Committee expects inflation

04:16.430 --> 04:20.110
to move gradually back
toward its objective.

04:20.110 --> 04:23.840
In making this forecast,
the Committee is mindful

04:23.840 --> 04:27.090
of the recent declines
in market-based measures

04:27.090 --> 04:29.810
of inflation compensation.

04:29.810 --> 04:33.270
At this point, the Committee
views these movements as likely

04:33.270 --> 04:37.110
to prove transitory, and
survey-based measures

04:37.110 --> 04:39.960
of longer-term inflation
expectations have

04:39.960 --> 04:42.080
remained stable.

04:42.080 --> 04:45.470
That said, developments in
this area obviously bear

04:45.470 --> 04:47.650
close watching.

04:47.650 --> 04:49.300
This outlook is reflected

04:49.300 --> 04:52.460
in the individual economic
projections submitted

04:52.460 --> 04:58.110
in conjunction with this meeting
by the FOMC participants.

04:58.110 --> 05:01.670
As always, each participant's
projections are conditioned

05:01.670 --> 05:07.210
on his or her own view of
appropriate monetary policy.

05:07.210 --> 05:08.490
The central tendency

05:08.490 --> 05:12.280
of the unemployment rate
projections is slightly lower

05:12.280 --> 05:16.110
than in the September
projections and now stands

05:16.110 --> 05:21.670
at 5.2 to 5.3 percent
at the end of next year,

05:21.670 --> 05:26.450
in line with its estimated
longer-run normal level.

05:26.450 --> 05:29.660
Committee participants generally
see the unemployment rate

05:29.660 --> 05:34.890
declining a little further over
the course of 2016 and 2017.

05:34.890 --> 05:37.550
The central tendency
of the projections

05:37.550 --> 05:44.500
for real GDP growth is 2.3
to 2.4 percent for 2014,

05:44.500 --> 05:48.020
up a bit from the
September projections.

05:48.020 --> 05:51.050
Over the next three
years, the projections

05:51.050 --> 05:56.440
for real GDP growth run
somewhat above the estimates

05:56.440 --> 06:00.210
of longer-run normal growth.

06:00.210 --> 06:04.550
Finally, although FOMC
participants project inflation

06:04.550 --> 06:08.470
in the near term to be lower
on account of the decline

06:08.470 --> 06:11.230
in energy prices, they continue

06:11.230 --> 06:15.350
to see inflation moving
gradually back toward 2 percent.

06:15.350 --> 06:19.950
The central tendency of the
inflation projections is 1.0

06:19.950 --> 06:28.220
to 1.6 percent next year, rising
to 1.8 to 2.0 percent in 2017.

06:28.220 --> 06:31.510
As I noted earlier, the
Committee "reaffirmed its view

06:31.510 --> 06:35.280
that the current 0 to one
quarter percent target range

06:35.280 --> 06:39.280
for the federal funds
rate remains appropriate."

06:39.280 --> 06:42.930
Regarding forward guidance
for the federal funds rate,

06:42.930 --> 06:45.830
our October statement indicated

06:45.830 --> 06:47.940
that it likely would
be appropriate

06:47.940 --> 06:52.000
to maintain the current target
range for the federal funds rate

06:52.000 --> 06:55.480
for a considerable
time following the end

06:55.480 --> 06:58.030
of our asset purchase program,

06:58.030 --> 07:01.280
especially if projected
inflation continues

07:01.280 --> 07:06.270
to run below the Committee's
2 percent longer-run goal.

07:06.270 --> 07:09.290
Today's statement,
which indicates that

07:09.290 --> 07:12.740
"the Committee judges that it
can be patient in beginning

07:12.740 --> 07:16.430
to normalize the stance
of monetary policy,"

07:16.430 --> 07:18.520
does not signify any change

07:18.520 --> 07:22.120
in the Committee's policy
intentions as set forth

07:22.120 --> 07:24.760
in its recent statements.

07:24.760 --> 07:27.150
As before, this judgment
is based

07:27.150 --> 07:30.090
on the Committee's
assessment of realized

07:30.090 --> 07:35.080
and expected progress toward its
objectives of maximum employment

07:35.080 --> 07:38.810
and 2 percent inflation-an
assessment that is based

07:38.810 --> 07:42.170
on a wide range of
information, including measures

07:42.170 --> 07:44.290
of labor market conditions,

07:44.290 --> 07:46.990
indicators of inflation
pressures

07:46.990 --> 07:49.770
and inflation expectations,
and readings

07:49.770 --> 07:52.780
on financial developments.

07:52.780 --> 07:57.300
Given that the Committee is not
signaling a change in policy,

07:57.300 --> 08:00.630
why did we update our guidance?

08:00.630 --> 08:05.310
The reason is that, with the
asset purchase program having

08:05.310 --> 08:10.930
been wound down in October, it
seemed less helpful to continue

08:10.930 --> 08:14.660
to communicate about
the possible timing

08:14.660 --> 08:19.090
of our first rate increase
with reference to an event

08:19.090 --> 08:22.560
that is receding into the past.

08:22.560 --> 08:25.090
Instead, we have
shifted to language

08:25.090 --> 08:28.240
that better reflects
the Committee's focus

08:28.240 --> 08:33.380
on the economic conditions that
would make liftoff appropriate.

08:33.380 --> 08:35.930
Employment is rising
at a healthy rate

08:35.930 --> 08:39.970
and the U.S. economy is
strengthening, reflecting,

08:39.970 --> 08:45.210
in part, a highly accommodative
stance of monetary policy.

08:45.210 --> 08:48.580
Of course, inflation has been
running somewhat below our goal

08:48.580 --> 08:52.310
of 2 percent, but
we project that gap

08:52.310 --> 08:55.420
to close gradually over time.

08:55.420 --> 08:58.090
As progress in achieving
maximum employment

08:58.090 --> 09:01.400
and 2 percent inflation
continues,

09:01.400 --> 09:03.750
at some point it will
become appropriate

09:03.750 --> 09:07.510
to begin reducing
policy accommodation.

09:07.510 --> 09:10.910
But, based on its current
outlook, the Committee judges

09:10.910 --> 09:14.950
that it can be patient
in doing so.

09:14.950 --> 09:19.720
In particular, the Committee
considers it unlikely

09:19.720 --> 09:22.770
to begin the normalization
process

09:22.770 --> 09:27.150
for at least the next
couple of meetings.

09:27.150 --> 09:32.310
This assessment, of course,
is completely data dependent.

09:32.310 --> 09:36.100
If incoming information
indicates faster progress toward

09:36.100 --> 09:39.870
the Committee's employment
and inflation objectives

09:39.870 --> 09:43.420
than the Committee now
expects, then increases

09:43.420 --> 09:46.920
in the target range for the
federal funds rate are likely

09:46.920 --> 09:51.200
to occur sooner than
currently anticipated.

09:51.200 --> 09:55.130
Conversely, if progress
proves slower than expected,

09:55.130 --> 09:58.690
then increases in the target
range are likely to occur later

09:58.690 --> 10:02.030
than currently anticipated.

10:02.030 --> 10:06.220
Once we begin to remove policy
accommodation, it continues

10:06.220 --> 10:10.060
to be the Committee's assessment
that, "even after employment

10:10.060 --> 10:14.300
and inflation are near
mandate-consistent levels,

10:14.300 --> 10:17.870
economic conditions
may, for some time,

10:17.870 --> 10:22.280
warrant keeping the target
federal funds rate below levels

10:22.280 --> 10:26.790
the Committee views as
normal in the longer run."

10:26.790 --> 10:29.710
This guidance is
consistent with the paths

10:29.710 --> 10:34.980
for appropriate policy
given by FOMC participants.

10:34.980 --> 10:38.050
Assuming that the economy
evolves broadly in line

10:38.050 --> 10:43.450
with participants' expectations,
almost all participants believe

10:43.450 --> 10:47.610
that it will be appropriate to
begin raising the target range

10:47.610 --> 10:52.150
for the federal funds
rate in 2015.

10:52.150 --> 10:55.390
There are a range of views
on the appropriate timing

10:55.390 --> 11:00.260
of liftoff within the year,
in part reflecting differences

11:00.260 --> 11:05.990
in participants' expectations
for how the economy will evolve.

11:05.990 --> 11:09.840
By the time of liftoff,
participants expect

11:09.840 --> 11:14.290
to see some further decline
in the unemployment rate

11:14.290 --> 11:18.800
and additional improvement
in labor market conditions.

11:18.800 --> 11:21.710
They also expect core inflation

11:21.710 --> 11:25.010
to be running near
current levels

11:25.010 --> 11:30.000
but foresee being reasonably
confident in their expectation

11:30.000 --> 11:33.760
that inflation will move back
toward our 2 percent longer-run

11:33.760 --> 11:37.740
inflation objective over time.

11:37.740 --> 11:40.790
Of course, as I previously
emphasized,

11:40.790 --> 11:42.830
the timing of the initial rise

11:42.830 --> 11:46.670
in the federal funds rate
target, as well as the path

11:46.670 --> 11:49.800
for the target thereafter,
are contingent

11:49.800 --> 11:52.940
on economic conditions.

11:52.940 --> 11:56.310
By late 2016, the
median projection

11:56.310 --> 12:01.730
for the federal funds rate,
at 2.5 percent, remains more

12:01.730 --> 12:05.400
than 1 percentage point below
the longer-run value of 3

12:05.400 --> 12:11.260
and three quarters percent or so
projected by most participants,

12:11.260 --> 12:14.260
even though the central tendency
of the unemployment rate

12:14.260 --> 12:19.850
by that time is slightly below
its estimated longer-run value

12:19.850 --> 12:22.540
and the central tendency
for inflation is close

12:22.540 --> 12:24.950
to our 2 percent objective.

12:24.950 --> 12:29.150
FOMC participants provide
a number of explanations

12:29.150 --> 12:32.920
for the federal funds rate
running below its normal

12:32.920 --> 12:39.120
longer-run level at that time-in
particular, the residual effects

12:39.120 --> 12:42.710
of the financial crisis,
which are likely to continue

12:42.710 --> 12:45.450
to constrain household spending

12:45.450 --> 12:49.860
and constrain credit
availability for some time.

12:49.860 --> 12:52.570
But as these factors
dissipate further,

12:52.570 --> 12:56.810
most participants expect the
federal funds rate to move close

12:56.810 --> 13:02.110
to its longer-run normal
level by the end of 2017.

13:02.110 --> 13:05.310
Finally, the Committee
will continue its policy

13:05.310 --> 13:10.300
of reinvesting proceeds from
maturing Treasury securities

13:10.300 --> 13:16.610
and principal payments from
holdings of agency debt and MBS.

13:16.610 --> 13:18.890
The Committee's sizable holdings

13:18.890 --> 13:22.990
of longer-term securities should
help maintain accommodative

13:22.990 --> 13:27.190
financial conditions and promote
further progress toward our

13:27.190 --> 13:32.300
objectives of maximum employment
and inflation of 2 percent.

13:32.300 --> 13:36.580
Thank you, and I'll be happy
to take your questions.

13:36.580 --> 13:37.770
JON HILSENRATH.

13:37.770 --> 13:45.020
Jon Hilsenrath from the
Wall Street Journal.

13:45.020 --> 13:48.000
Chair Yellen, a number of
Fed officials have projected

13:48.000 --> 13:51.410
in the lead-up to this meeting
that the most likely timing

13:51.410 --> 13:55.120
for liftoff was around
the middle of next year.

13:55.120 --> 13:57.090
I wonder if you could
clarify that.

13:57.090 --> 14:00.540
You said in your statement
that "patient" means not

14:00.540 --> 14:02.400
for at least two meetings.

14:02.400 --> 14:06.620
Your forecast that-the FOMC's
forecasts seem to be consistent

14:06.620 --> 14:09.810
with something like a
middle-of-the-year liftoff.

14:09.810 --> 14:10.770
Can you speak to that?

14:10.770 --> 14:13.950
And can you also speak to
the downdraft we're seeing

14:13.950 --> 14:16.020
in inflation now
and, in particular,

14:16.020 --> 14:19.690
the market-based inflation
expectations and whether

14:19.690 --> 14:22.980
that gives the Committee
any hesitance

14:22.980 --> 14:26.290
about proceeding towards
liftoff in the months ahead?

14:26.290 --> 14:26.700
CHAIR YELLEN.

14:26.700 --> 14:32.140
So I did say that the statement

14:32.140 --> 14:37.310
that the Committee can be
patient should be interpreted

14:37.310 --> 14:39.300
as meaning that it is unlikely

14:39.300 --> 14:42.120
to begin the normalization
process

14:42.120 --> 14:45.800
for at least the next
couple of meetings.

14:45.800 --> 14:50.920
Now, that doesn't point to any
preset or predetermined time

14:50.920 --> 14:56.880
at which normalization
is-will begin.

14:56.880 --> 14:59.990
There are a range of
views on the Committee,

14:59.990 --> 15:06.440
and it will be dependent
on how incoming data bear

15:06.440 --> 15:10.630
on the progress the
economy is making.

15:10.630 --> 15:13.380
First of all, I want
to emphasize

15:13.380 --> 15:17.680
that no meeting is completely
off the table, in the sense

15:17.680 --> 15:22.090
that if we do see faster
progress toward our objectives

15:22.090 --> 15:26.450
than we currently expect, then
it is possible that the process

15:26.450 --> 15:33.600
of normalization would occur
sooner than we now anticipate.

15:33.600 --> 15:37.380
And, of course, the
converse is also true.

15:37.380 --> 15:42.610
So, at this point,
we think it unlikely

15:42.610 --> 15:46.690
that it will be appropriate-that
we will see conditions

15:46.690 --> 15:49.650
for at least the next
couple of meetings

15:49.650 --> 15:54.790
that will make it
appropriate-for us to decide

15:54.790 --> 15:57.140
to begin normalization.

15:57.140 --> 16:01.070
A number of Committee
participants have indicated

16:01.070 --> 16:04.780
that, in their view,
conditions could be appropriate

16:04.780 --> 16:09.150
by the middle of next year,
but there is no preset time.

16:09.150 --> 16:11.920
And there are a range
of views as to

16:11.920 --> 16:16.100
when the appropriate conditions
will likely fall in place.

16:16.100 --> 16:19.200
So that's something we
will be watching closely

16:19.200 --> 16:21.710
as the year unfolds.

16:21.710 --> 16:24.550
You asked also, I
think, about inflation.

16:24.550 --> 16:28.130
And, as I mentioned
in my press statement,

16:28.130 --> 16:32.020
especially with the downward
pressures on inflation

16:32.020 --> 16:35.560
that we expect to see
for a little while

16:35.560 --> 16:41.540
because of declining oil prices
and falling import prices,

16:41.540 --> 16:45.600
we certainly expect
headline inflation to be

16:45.600 --> 16:48.240
under downward pressure
for a while.

16:48.240 --> 16:52.700
And, as I mentioned, most
participants do envision

16:52.700 --> 16:56.240
that conditions will be
appropriate sometime during this

16:56.240 --> 16:59.960
coming year to begin
normalizing policy.

16:59.960 --> 17:02.870
And they do largely expect

17:02.870 --> 17:07.900
that inflation will be-core
inflation will probably be

17:07.900 --> 17:12.550
running close to
its current level.

17:12.550 --> 17:15.820
And headline inflation
could even be lower.

17:15.820 --> 17:19.630
But what they will want
to have is a feeling

17:19.630 --> 17:23.180
of reasonable confidence that
when we start the process

17:23.180 --> 17:27.280
of normalizing policy, that it
will be moving up over time.

17:27.280 --> 17:32.760
And, of course, as labor market
conditions continue to improve,

17:32.760 --> 17:35.160
history suggests that as long

17:35.160 --> 17:38.790
as inflation expectations
remain well anchored,

17:38.790 --> 17:41.600
that that's likely to occur.

17:41.600 --> 17:46.010
MARTIN CRUTSINGER.

17:46.010 --> 17:48.710
Marty Crutsinger with
the Associated Press.

17:48.710 --> 17:53.970
Given the-what's happening
now with the transition

17:53.970 --> 17:58.090
with the Fed, there seems
to be a pattern developing

17:58.090 --> 18:01.950
that the market expects
big news to come

18:01.950 --> 18:04.590
when you have a press
conference and no news to come

18:04.590 --> 18:06.490
when you don't have one.

18:06.490 --> 18:11.320
But is that a good expectation?

18:11.320 --> 18:13.170
And is there any
thought to starting

18:13.170 --> 18:15.010
to have a press conference
at every meeting?

18:15.010 --> 18:16.300
CHAIR YELLEN.

18:16.300 --> 18:21.520
So I would really like to
discourage that expectation.

18:23.270 --> 18:26.360
Every meeting that we
have is a live meeting

18:26.360 --> 18:30.290
at which the Committee could
make a policy decision.

18:30.290 --> 18:34.140
And we will feel free to do so.

18:34.140 --> 18:37.230
So I would really like to
strongly discourage the

18:37.230 --> 18:41.800
expectation that policy
moves can only occur

18:41.800 --> 18:44.460
when there's a scheduled
press conference.

18:44.460 --> 18:48.200
And we have long had
in place the ability

18:48.200 --> 18:53.610
to hold a press conference call,

18:53.610 --> 18:57.200
rather than an in-person
press conference.

18:57.200 --> 19:02.140
And we did do so on a number
of occasions in earlier years.

19:02.140 --> 19:06.990
So the Committee, clearly,
would want to be able

19:06.990 --> 19:09.580
to explain its reasoning.

19:09.580 --> 19:13.760
As we begin the process
of normalizing policy,

19:13.760 --> 19:15.700
every meeting is live.

19:15.700 --> 19:20.420
And if we were to decide
at a meeting to begin

19:20.420 --> 19:24.530
to normalize policy, I
expect we would hold a press

19:24.530 --> 19:26.250
conference call.

19:26.250 --> 19:28.960
STEVE LIESMAN.

19:28.960 --> 19:34.590
Was there concern-Steve Liesman,
CNBC-was there concern expressed

19:34.590 --> 19:36.770
at the meeting that
the signal coming

19:36.770 --> 19:40.250
from markets-and a variety
of markets-lower oil prices,

19:40.250 --> 19:43.520
lower yields around the
world, was one of deflation,

19:43.520 --> 19:47.230
and that that risk was
one that should, perhaps,

19:47.230 --> 19:50.610
overshadow the concern about
inflation on the other side?

19:50.610 --> 19:51.600
CHAIR YELLEN.

19:51.600 --> 19:52.800
Well, thanks Steve.

19:52.800 --> 19:56.800
We're very attentive
to global developments

19:56.800 --> 20:02.150
and certainly discuss
them in the meeting.

20:02.150 --> 20:07.880
The very substantial decline we
have seen in oil prices is one

20:07.880 --> 20:11.480
of the most important
developments shaping the

20:11.480 --> 20:13.850
global outlook.

20:13.850 --> 20:17.230
It will have different
effects in different regions

20:17.230 --> 20:19.420
and could well have effects

20:19.420 --> 20:23.510
on financial markets,
as we are seeing.

20:23.510 --> 20:26.570
I think the judgment of
the Committee is that,

20:26.570 --> 20:28.940
from the standpoint
of the United States

20:28.940 --> 20:32.810
and the U.S. outlook, that
the decline we have seen

20:32.810 --> 20:36.720
in oil prices is likely
to be, on net, a positive.

20:36.720 --> 20:39.710
It's something that's
certainly good

20:39.710 --> 20:42.170
for families, for households.

20:42.170 --> 20:45.350
It's putting more
money in their pockets.

20:45.350 --> 20:50.510
Having to spend less on gas and
energy, and so, in that sense,

20:50.510 --> 20:54.920
it's like a tax cut that
boosts their spending power.

20:54.920 --> 20:58.480
The United States
remains-although our production

20:58.480 --> 21:01.000
of oil has increased
dramatically,

21:01.000 --> 21:04.700
we still remain a
net importer of oil.

21:04.700 --> 21:07.900
Of course there may be
some offset in the form

21:07.900 --> 21:13.580
of reduced drilling activity,
and possibly some change,

21:13.580 --> 21:19.310
some reduction in cap-ex
plans in the drilling area.

21:19.310 --> 21:24.960
But, on balance, I would see
these developments as a positive

21:24.960 --> 21:29.380
for the standpoint
of the U.S. economy.

21:29.380 --> 21:37.080
With respect to deflation,
we see downward pressure

21:37.080 --> 21:42.500
on headline inflation from
declining energy prices.

21:42.500 --> 21:46.090
We certainly recognize that
that is going to be pushing

21:46.090 --> 21:49.270
down headline inflation
and may even spill over,

21:49.270 --> 21:52.470
to some extent, to
core inflation.

21:52.470 --> 21:55.810
But, at this point, although
we indicated we're monitoring

21:55.810 --> 21:58.960
inflation developments
carefully,

21:58.960 --> 22:01.860
we see these developments
as transitory.

22:01.860 --> 22:07.180
And the Committee continues
to believe-especially

22:07.180 --> 22:10.260
with the improvement we're
seeing in the labor market,

22:10.260 --> 22:14.240
which we expect to continue-that
inflation will move back

22:14.240 --> 22:16.640
up to our 2 percent
objective over time.

22:16.640 --> 22:20.100
As I indicated, we will
want to feel, I believe,

22:20.100 --> 22:24.080
that people will expect to feel
reasonably confident about that

22:24.080 --> 22:27.570
when they-when the process
of normalization begins.

22:27.570 --> 22:30.630
But we do expect them
to be transitory.

22:30.630 --> 22:31.880
ROBIN HARDING.

22:31.880 --> 22:37.350
Robin Harding from
the Financial Times.

22:37.350 --> 22:39.030
Madame Chair, there's a big gap

22:39.030 --> 22:42.540
between the pace markets expect
you to raise interest rates

22:42.540 --> 22:45.960
and the rate you've
indicated in your "dot plot."

22:45.960 --> 22:48.400
Are markets misunderstanding
your intentions?

22:48.400 --> 22:50.350
Thank you.

22:50.350 --> 22:50.790
CHAIR YELLEN.

22:50.790 --> 22:52.350
So that's difficult
for me to say.

22:52.350 --> 22:55.950
What I want to say is that our
objective is to communicate

22:55.950 --> 23:00.150
as clearly as we possibly
can about our plans

23:00.150 --> 23:05.270
and how we see the economic
environment unfolding.

23:05.270 --> 23:08.630
When the participants
in the Committee fill

23:08.630 --> 23:15.280
out their projections,
they're asked to give the path

23:15.280 --> 23:21.620
of the federal funds rate and of
the various economic variables

23:21.620 --> 23:24.720
that they consider most likely.

23:24.720 --> 23:29.040
They're not asked to talk
about all the different things

23:29.040 --> 23:34.040
that could happen, recognizing
there is uncertainty,

23:34.040 --> 23:36.490
and the paths of the funds rate

23:36.490 --> 23:39.260
that they would consider
appropriate

23:39.260 --> 23:42.410
if those other alternatives
were to happen.

23:42.410 --> 23:47.220
But other alternatives, I think,
are priced into the market.

23:47.220 --> 23:51.630
And one reason that the
market prices may be different

23:51.630 --> 23:55.830
than the Committee's is
because they place probability

23:55.830 --> 24:00.830
on other outcomes that look
different than what they regard

24:00.830 --> 24:02.710
as the modal forecast.

24:02.710 --> 24:06.650
They may also have a
different set of expectations

24:06.650 --> 24:10.500
about how the-about
the economic outlook

24:10.500 --> 24:12.880
and how it's likely to unfold.

24:12.880 --> 24:16.730
So I recognize that there
are significant differences.

24:16.730 --> 24:19.790
I can't tell you exactly
what they're due to.

24:19.790 --> 24:25.120
But what I do want to do is
communicate as clearly as I can,

24:25.120 --> 24:29.470
on behalf of the Committee, how
we think the economy's likely

24:29.470 --> 24:34.200
to progress and how we would
likely set the federal funds

24:34.200 --> 24:37.380
rate over time if that
forecast bears out.

24:37.380 --> 24:38.010
ROBIN HARDING.

24:38.010 --> 24:39.960
It doesn't make you
uncomfortable,

24:39.960 --> 24:42.720
where markets are now?

24:42.720 --> 24:44.310
CHAIR YELLEN.

24:44.310 --> 24:47.970
There are a number of different
factors that are bearing

24:47.970 --> 24:52.580
on the path of market
interest rates, I think,

24:52.580 --> 24:55.920
including global
economic developments.

24:55.920 --> 25:00.020
It is often the case that
when oil prices move down

25:00.020 --> 25:03.630
and the dollar appreciates,
that that tends

25:03.630 --> 25:07.300
to put downward pressure
on inflation compensation

25:07.300 --> 25:09.380
and on longer-term rates.

25:09.380 --> 25:12.010
We also have safe-haven flows

25:12.010 --> 25:15.440
that may be affecting
longer-term Treasury yields.

25:15.440 --> 25:22.980
So I can't tell you exactly what
is driving market developments.

25:22.980 --> 25:26.100
But what I can say
is that we are trying

25:26.100 --> 25:30.540
to communicate our thoughts
as clearly as we can.

25:30.540 --> 25:34.500
STEVEN MUFSON.

25:34.500 --> 25:36.460
Hi. Steve Mufson from
the Washington Post.

25:36.460 --> 25:38.560
I was just hoping you could go

25:38.560 --> 25:40.970
into a little more detail
about the oil effect.

25:40.970 --> 25:43.090
Even though you see
it as transitory,

25:43.090 --> 25:45.690
does that give you a little
more room to keep rates low

25:45.690 --> 25:46.950
in the next few months?

25:46.950 --> 25:50.880
And if-alternately, if prices
bounce back, what's that going

25:50.880 --> 25:52.920
to do to your ability
to change rates,

25:52.920 --> 25:55.630
and how might you react to that?

25:55.630 --> 25:56.640
CHAIR YELLEN.

25:56.640 --> 26:01.810
Well I-I'd say, you know,
that I think what we have seen

26:01.810 --> 26:06.220
since the mid-'80s is
that, in an environment

26:06.220 --> 26:11.260
where inflation expectations are
well anchored, that movements

26:11.260 --> 26:16.770
in oil and commodity prices
and import prices tend

26:16.770 --> 26:20.700
to have transitory effects
on the inflation outlook.

26:20.700 --> 26:27.000
There were many years in which
we had unanticipated increases

26:27.000 --> 26:33.570
in oil prices, really
beginning in 2004 and 2005,

26:33.570 --> 26:36.990
that put upward pressure
on headline inflation

26:36.990 --> 26:40.990
and sometimes even spilled
through into core, and,

26:40.990 --> 26:45.720
typically, the Committee
looked through those impacts

26:45.720 --> 26:49.660
on inflation with the view
that they would be transitory.

26:49.660 --> 26:54.790
And I think experience bears
out that they were transitory.

26:54.790 --> 26:59.660
And I think that's the
Committee's expectation here.

26:59.660 --> 27:01.770
Inflation, even core inflation,

27:01.770 --> 27:06.460
has been running below
our inflation objective.

27:06.460 --> 27:10.460
Movements in oil, you know,
now down and perhaps later up,

27:10.460 --> 27:15.710
will move inflation around,
certainly headline inflation.

27:15.710 --> 27:17.630
But the Committee,
at this point,

27:17.630 --> 27:21.150
anticipates those
impacts to be transitory.

27:21.150 --> 27:26.710
So as long as participants
feel reasonably confident

27:26.710 --> 27:30.910
that the inflation projection
is one where we expect

27:30.910 --> 27:35.850
to meet our 2 percent
objective over time,

27:35.850 --> 27:38.740
that's what I think how
they'll be looking at things

27:38.740 --> 27:42.520
as we decide on the
path for the funds rate.

27:42.520 --> 27:46.750
BINYAMIN APPELBAUM.

27:46.750 --> 27:48.500
Binya Appelbaum, New York Times.

27:48.500 --> 27:50.360
Does "a couple" mean two?

27:50.360 --> 27:52.910
And when you talk about
reasonable confidence

27:52.910 --> 27:55.920
in inflation expectations,
can you elaborate a little bit

27:55.920 --> 27:58.400
about what it would take to
give you reasonable confidence

27:58.400 --> 28:01.810
that inflation is
headed back to 2 percent?

28:01.810 --> 28:02.270
CHAIR YELLEN.

28:02.270 --> 28:04.990
So "a couple"-I believe the
dictionary probably says,

28:04.990 --> 28:06.710
"a couple" means two.

28:06.710 --> 28:09.130
So, "a couple" means two.

28:09.130 --> 28:15.100
And with respect to
inflation-our forecast

28:15.100 --> 28:19.770
for inflation and inflation
expectations, let me start

28:19.770 --> 28:22.030
by saying I think it's important

28:22.030 --> 28:25.250
that monetary policy
be forward looking.

28:25.250 --> 28:30.020
The lags in monetary policy
are long, and, therefore,

28:30.020 --> 28:33.820
the Committee has to
base its decisions on how

28:33.820 --> 28:38.070
to set the federal funds
rate looking into the future.

28:40.220 --> 28:44.760
Theory is important, and
theories that are consistent

28:44.760 --> 28:47.510
with historical evidence
will be something

28:47.510 --> 28:52.450
that governs the thinking of
many people around the table.

28:52.450 --> 28:55.620
Typically, we have
seen that as long

28:55.620 --> 28:58.790
as inflation expectations
are well anchored,

28:58.790 --> 29:02.130
that as the labor
market recovers,

29:02.130 --> 29:07.070
we'll gradually see upward
pressure on both wages

29:07.070 --> 29:10.730
and prices, and that
inflation will tend

29:10.730 --> 29:13.150
to move back toward 2 percent.

29:13.150 --> 29:18.770
I think historically we have
seen, as the economy strengthens

29:18.770 --> 29:22.450
and slack diminishes,
that inflation does tend

29:22.450 --> 29:25.110
to gradually rise over time.

29:25.110 --> 29:31.620
And as long-you know,
I-just speaking for myself,

29:31.620 --> 29:36.920
I will be looking for evidence

29:36.920 --> 29:41.880
that I think strengthens my
confidence in that view and,

29:41.880 --> 29:47.090
you know, looking at the
full range of data that bear

29:47.090 --> 29:49.310
on whether or not
that's a reasonable view

29:49.310 --> 29:51.210
of how events will unfold.

29:51.210 --> 29:55.600
But it's likely to be a decision
that's based on forecasts

29:55.600 --> 29:57.410
and confidence in the forecast.

29:57.410 --> 30:02.230
MICHAEL FLAHERTY.

30:02.230 --> 30:04.460
Michael Flaherty, Reuters News.

30:04.460 --> 30:07.500
Chair Yellen, a lot of attention
has been focused on liftoff.

30:07.500 --> 30:11.370
But, I wonder, has the
Committee discussed what happens

30:11.370 --> 30:16.920
after that liftoff and what,
you know, the rate path would be

30:16.920 --> 30:17.940
after you make the first move?

30:17.940 --> 30:18.930
CHAIR YELLEN.

30:18.930 --> 30:22.820
So, I think you raise a very
important point because,

30:22.820 --> 30:27.190
although there is a great deal
of market focus on the timing

30:27.190 --> 30:32.780
of liftoff, what to matter
in thinking about the stance

30:32.780 --> 30:35.960
of policy is what
the entire path

30:35.960 --> 30:39.090
of interest rates
will look like.

30:39.090 --> 30:43.060
And I really don't have much
for you other than to say

30:43.060 --> 30:46.520
that they will be data
dependent-that, over time,

30:46.520 --> 30:50.830
the stance of policy
will be adjusted to try

30:50.830 --> 30:53.540
to keep the economy on a track

30:53.540 --> 30:58.540
where we see continuing progress
toward achieving our goals

30:58.540 --> 31:03.380
of maximum employment
and price stability.

31:03.380 --> 31:07.940
There's-you know, the federal
funds rate has been sitting

31:07.940 --> 31:13.150
in the 0 to a quarter percent
range now for six years.

31:13.150 --> 31:16.190
This is-and we have a
very large balance sheet.

31:16.190 --> 31:22.790
We are providing a very highly
accommodative monetary policy.

31:22.790 --> 31:25.750
And even as we begin
to normalize the stance

31:25.750 --> 31:29.550
of monetary policy, when
that becomes appropriate,

31:29.550 --> 31:31.130
it's important to remember

31:31.130 --> 31:36.140
that monetary policy will
still be very accommodative

31:36.140 --> 31:37.960
for a long time.

31:37.960 --> 31:42.480
And as we begin to normalize
policy, we will be looking

31:42.480 --> 31:45.230
at unfolding economic
developments.

31:45.230 --> 31:49.000
And as the economy
strengthens, and we come closer

31:49.000 --> 31:56.710
to achieving our objectives,
I think it's very likely

31:56.710 --> 31:59.980
that we will, you know,
progress on the path

31:59.980 --> 32:03.580
of normalizing policy.

32:03.580 --> 32:06.710
But I can't tell
you, specifically,

32:06.710 --> 32:09.700
other than saying it
will depend on progress,

32:09.700 --> 32:12.120
and moves will be
data dependent.

32:12.120 --> 32:14.760
I can't say much more than that.

32:14.760 --> 32:17.590
GREG IP. Chair Yellen,

32:17.590 --> 32:21.960
the Committee's projections show
unemployment running below your

32:21.960 --> 32:23.480
own views on where full
employment should be

32:23.480 --> 32:25.140
for the next several years.

32:25.140 --> 32:27.550
Does that reflect a desire
on the part of the Committee

32:27.550 --> 32:30.210
that the economy run somewhat
above potential for a while?

32:30.210 --> 32:32.900
And, if so, can you
elaborate on why it wants that

32:32.900 --> 32:34.550
and what purpose it achieves?

32:34.550 --> 32:35.550
And, related to the question

32:35.550 --> 32:37.840
that Jon Hilsenrath
asked earlier,

32:37.840 --> 32:38.720
you've called the decline

32:38.720 --> 32:40.090
in inflation-and
market-based measures

32:40.090 --> 32:42.140
of inflation
expectations-transitory.

32:42.140 --> 32:43.920
But this decline has
been very pronounced

32:43.920 --> 32:45.540
in the five-year-forward range.

32:45.540 --> 32:47.460
So we're talking
about expectations

32:47.460 --> 32:49.990
that inflation many years
from now will be below target.

32:49.990 --> 32:52.840
And some market participants
see that as evidence

32:52.840 --> 32:56.920
of declining credibility in the
Committee's long-term objective.

32:56.920 --> 33:00.860
Why do you still view
that as transitory?

33:00.860 --> 33:01.380
CHAIR YELLEN.

33:01.380 --> 33:06.500
So you-your first question is:

33:06.500 --> 33:09.840
Why is it that the
Committee sees unemployment

33:09.840 --> 33:13.230
as declining slightly
below its estimate

33:13.230 --> 33:17.290
of the longer-run natural rate?

33:17.290 --> 33:20.720
And, I think, in part,
the reason for that is

33:20.720 --> 33:25.560
that inflation is running
below our objective,

33:25.560 --> 33:27.500
and the Committee wants

33:27.500 --> 33:33.180
to see inflation move back
toward our objective over time.

33:33.180 --> 33:38.930
And a short period of a
very slight undershoot

33:38.930 --> 33:44.480
of unemployment below the
natural rate will facilitate a

33:44.480 --> 33:49.820
slightly faster return of
inflation to our objective.

33:49.820 --> 33:57.320
It is, I should say, a very
small undershoot in a situation

33:57.320 --> 33:59.650
where there is great uncertainty

33:59.650 --> 34:05.030
about exactly what
constitutes maximum employment

34:05.030 --> 34:08.280
or a longer-run normal
rate of unemployment.

34:08.280 --> 34:13.330
We also do see the
different measures of slack

34:13.330 --> 34:18.580
in the labor market point
to different assessments

34:18.580 --> 34:23.330
of just what maximum
employment is.

34:23.330 --> 34:28.300
The standard unemployment
rate, for quite some time now,

34:28.300 --> 34:34.990
has been signaling a little bit
less slack in the labor market

34:34.990 --> 34:38.810
than measures that are somewhat
broader that, for example,

34:38.810 --> 34:43.220
include the unusually
large number of people

34:43.220 --> 34:49.840
who were part-time employed but
would prefer full-time jobs.

34:49.840 --> 34:53.990
And the portion of
the decline we've seen

34:53.990 --> 34:56.370
in labor force participation,

34:56.370 --> 35:00.800
that looks like it would
disappear in a-or be eroded

35:00.800 --> 35:03.400
in a stronger economy.

35:03.400 --> 35:07.270
And so it may be that with
a very small undershoot

35:07.270 --> 35:12.220
of this longer-run normal
level of the unemployment rate,

35:12.220 --> 35:15.090
as measured by the
standard unemployment rate,

35:15.090 --> 35:17.600
we'll be seeing some
further progress

35:17.600 --> 35:20.020
on those other margins of slack.

35:20.020 --> 35:21.450
But it's important to point

35:21.450 --> 35:25.430
out that the Committee is
not anticipating an overshoot

35:25.430 --> 35:28.720
of its 2 percent
inflation objective.

35:28.720 --> 35:34.380
Oh, and longer-dated
expectations.

35:34.380 --> 35:40.260
Well, what I would say, we
refer to this in the statement

35:40.260 --> 35:43.250
as "inflation compensation"
rather than

35:43.250 --> 35:45.130
"inflation expectations."

35:45.130 --> 35:51.820
The gap between the nominal
yields on 10-year Treasuries,

35:51.820 --> 35:56.180
for example, and TIPS
have declined-that's

35:56.180 --> 35:58.240
inflation compensation.

35:58.240 --> 36:00.090
And five-year,
five-year-forwards,

36:00.090 --> 36:02.920
as you've said, have
also declined.

36:02.920 --> 36:07.020
That could reflect a change
in inflation expectations,

36:07.020 --> 36:11.080
but it could also
reflect changes

36:11.080 --> 36:15.330
in assessment of
inflation risks.

36:15.330 --> 36:18.940
The risk premium that's
necessary to compensate

36:18.940 --> 36:22.550
for inflation, that might
especially have fallen

36:22.550 --> 36:24.810
if the probabilities attached

36:24.810 --> 36:27.850
to very high inflation
have come down.

36:27.850 --> 36:33.010
And it can also reflect
liquidity effects in markets.

36:33.010 --> 36:36.890
And, for example, it's sometimes
the case that-when there is a

36:36.890 --> 36:41.350
flight to safety, that flight
tends to be concentrated

36:41.350 --> 36:44.250
in nominal Treasuries
and could also serve

36:44.250 --> 36:46.030
to compress that spread.

36:46.030 --> 36:51.840
So I think the jury is out
about exactly how to interpret

36:51.840 --> 36:55.520
that downward move in
inflation compensation.

36:55.520 --> 36:59.040
And we indicated that we
are monitoring inflation

36:59.040 --> 37:02.130
developments carefully.

37:02.130 --> 37:03.440
PETER COOK.

37:03.440 --> 37:05.570
Madame Chair, Peter Cook
of Bloomberg Television.

37:05.570 --> 37:09.290
I want to follow up, if I
could, on firming going forward,

37:09.290 --> 37:11.980
on the normalization
once liftoff takes place.

37:11.980 --> 37:14.000
I know you said this is
going to be data dependent.

37:14.000 --> 37:16.900
Does that suggest to
markets, to those watching,

37:16.900 --> 37:19.130
that the measured
pace we've seen

37:19.130 --> 37:22.120
in a previous tightening cycle,
those quarter-point increments,

37:22.120 --> 37:24.420
that that's not something
markets should expect?

37:24.420 --> 37:26.730
And what's your own
takeaway from how effective

37:26.730 --> 37:30.130
that measured pace was back in
that previous tightening cycle?

37:30.130 --> 37:31.680
And if I could follow
up just separately

37:31.680 --> 37:32.980
on the dissents at this meeting.

37:32.980 --> 37:35.700
There were three dissents,
a notable number, certainly.

37:35.700 --> 37:38.460
What does that suggest about
the debate around the table

37:38.460 --> 37:40.820
and your ability to forge
consensus going forward?

37:40.820 --> 37:43.450
Are you disappointed with
the number of dissents?

37:43.450 --> 37:43.870
CHAIR YELLEN.

37:43.870 --> 37:49.100
So let me start with
the number of dissents.

37:50.250 --> 37:54.750
There is a wide range of
opinion in the Committee.

37:54.750 --> 37:58.790
I think it's appropriate
for people to be able

37:58.790 --> 38:01.110
to express their views.

38:01.110 --> 38:07.070
And, in a sense, you see
dissents on both sides.

38:07.070 --> 38:10.510
I think the statement
does a good job

38:10.510 --> 38:13.920
of reflecting what the majority

38:13.920 --> 38:19.330
of the Committee thinks
is appropriate policy.

38:19.330 --> 38:21.740
So at-you know, at
a time like this,

38:21.740 --> 38:25.120
where we are making
consequential decisions,

38:25.120 --> 38:31.510
I think it's very reasonable
to see divergences of opinion.

38:31.510 --> 38:34.050
And just remind me, what was
the other-about the- PETER COOK.

38:34.050 --> 38:34.470
Measured pace.

38:34.470 --> 38:34.710
CHAIR YELLEN.

38:34.710 --> 38:36.330
-measured pace.

38:36.330 --> 38:39.770
There certainly has been no,
you know, decision on the part

38:39.770 --> 38:42.980
of the Committee to
move at a measured pace

38:42.980 --> 38:45.810
or to use language like that.

38:45.810 --> 38:50.280
I think quite a few people,
looking back on the use

38:50.280 --> 38:53.930
of that language in the-I
can't remember if it was 12

38:53.930 --> 38:58.460
or 16 meetings, where there
were 25 basis point moves.

38:58.460 --> 39:02.870
We'd probably not like
to repeat a sequence

39:02.870 --> 39:05.230
in which there was
a measured pace

39:05.230 --> 39:08.330
and 25 basis point
moves at every meeting.

39:08.330 --> 39:11.280
So I certainly don't want
to encourage you to think

39:11.280 --> 39:15.480
that there will be
a repeat of that.

39:15.480 --> 39:19.260
Many members of the
Committee, participants,

39:19.260 --> 39:22.990
have said that they think
policy should be based

39:22.990 --> 39:28.690
on the actual evolution of
economic activity and inflation,

39:28.690 --> 39:31.690
which tends to be
variable over time,

39:31.690 --> 39:34.700
and that's why I say I
anticipate it will be

39:34.700 --> 39:36.350
data dependent.

39:36.350 --> 39:40.560
We have continued to provide
guidance, the same guidance

39:40.560 --> 39:44.940
that we have for some time,
that says the Committee

39:44.940 --> 39:47.610
"anticipates that,
even after employment

39:47.610 --> 39:51.550
and inflation are near
mandate-consistent levels,

39:51.550 --> 39:54.740
economic conditions
may, for some time,

39:54.740 --> 39:59.110
warrant keeping the target
federal funds rate below levels

39:59.110 --> 40:01.940
the Committee views as
normal in the longer run."

40:01.940 --> 40:05.280
I know that's a mouthful,
but it says, in effect,

40:05.280 --> 40:10.500
that the Committee believes
that the economic conditions

40:10.500 --> 40:13.250
that have made recovery
difficult,

40:13.250 --> 40:15.430
we're getting beyond them.

40:15.430 --> 40:19.530
They are optimistic that
those conditions will lift.

40:19.530 --> 40:23.170
They see the longer-run
normal level of interest rates

40:23.170 --> 40:27.640
as around 3 and three
quarter percent.

40:27.640 --> 40:31.530
So there's no view
in the Committee

40:31.530 --> 40:34.930
that there is secular
stagnation in the sense

40:34.930 --> 40:37.020
that we won't eventually
get back

40:37.020 --> 40:41.180
to pretty historically normal
levels of interest rates.

40:41.180 --> 40:46.210
But they have said, it'll, you
know, the economy has required

40:46.210 --> 40:48.970
to get where it is a good deal

40:48.970 --> 40:52.380
of monetary policy
accommodation;

40:52.380 --> 40:55.730
we expect to be able
to normalize policy.

40:55.730 --> 40:59.330
But, until those
conditions have lifted

40:59.330 --> 41:02.100
that have held back
economic activity,

41:02.100 --> 41:05.770
monetary policy will need
to stay accommodative.

41:05.770 --> 41:10.810
So, in that sense, perhaps
that's equivalent to saying

41:10.810 --> 41:14.360
that the path of
normalization is anticipated

41:14.360 --> 41:17.460
to be relatively gradual.

41:17.460 --> 41:20.860
But, again, the path
of rates will depend

41:20.860 --> 41:24.700
on how economic conditions
actually evolve,

41:24.700 --> 41:27.930
and that's nothing more
than an expectation

41:27.930 --> 41:30.060
on the part of the Committee.

41:30.060 --> 41:34.650
PEDRO DA COSTA.

41:34.650 --> 41:36.770
Pedro da Costa with
Dow Jones Newswires.

41:36.770 --> 41:38.010
Enough about rates.

41:38.010 --> 41:40.350
I want to ask you
about the New York Fed.

41:40.350 --> 41:42.480
The New York Fed's been
in the news a lot lately.

41:42.480 --> 41:47.350
President Dudley was invited
to Congress to testify

41:47.350 --> 41:49.830
about conflicts of
interest there.

41:49.830 --> 41:53.800
You had things like the Segarra
tapes, the Beim report, and,

41:53.800 --> 41:55.090
most recently, the revelation

41:55.090 --> 41:58.510
that a former New York Fed
official was exchanging

41:58.510 --> 42:01.080
information with
someone at Goldman Sachs

42:01.080 --> 42:04.310
who was also-had New
York Fed connections.

42:04.310 --> 42:07.710
I just wonder-and also there
were scandals during the crisis

42:07.710 --> 42:09.690
related to Stephen Friedman
regarding the New York Fed,

42:09.690 --> 42:12.120
and his purchase of
Goldman Sachs stock.

42:12.120 --> 42:15.190
Do you see the New York Fed as
a black mark on the Fed system

42:15.190 --> 42:16.820
because of these
recurring scandals?

42:16.820 --> 42:21.820
Have you talked to Bill Dudley
about reforming the image

42:21.820 --> 42:23.860
of that particular regional Fed?

42:23.860 --> 42:27.300
And do you think a person
that has-that spent 21 years

42:27.300 --> 42:30.400
of his career at Goldman
Sachs is in a position

42:30.400 --> 42:33.150
to regain public credibility
about conflicts of interest?

42:33.150 --> 42:34.050
CHAIR YELLEN.

42:34.050 --> 42:39.230
Well, let me say that I
think it's very important

42:39.230 --> 42:42.420
for the Federal Reserve
System to have confidence

42:42.420 --> 42:45.170
in the quality of
its supervision.

42:45.170 --> 42:50.870
And I do have a good deal
of confidence in the quality

42:50.870 --> 42:52.890
of our supervision program,

42:52.890 --> 42:57.680
for the banking organizations
we supervise in general,

42:57.680 --> 43:02.940
and that also applies to the
largest banking organizations.

43:02.940 --> 43:06.850
We rely on examiners
who are in the field

43:06.850 --> 43:11.620
and at the Reserve Banks
to be providing information

43:11.620 --> 43:14.720
about what's happening
in those organizations.

43:14.720 --> 43:18.170
But that information
feeds into a process

43:18.170 --> 43:24.790
in which it is not individuals
at any single Reserve Bank.

43:24.790 --> 43:28.320
But, at the Board, it's
a Board-led process,

43:28.320 --> 43:32.870
and it involves senior
officials at a number

43:32.870 --> 43:34.880
of different Reserve Banks.

43:34.880 --> 43:38.320
It's also a multidisciplinary
process

43:38.320 --> 43:41.300
that involves not only
people from supervision,

43:41.300 --> 43:46.040
but those from markets,
from economic research.

43:46.040 --> 43:51.750
Experts who focus on financial
stability all come together

43:51.750 --> 43:56.670
to evaluate the information
that they have

43:56.670 --> 44:03.130
and to assign supervisory
ratings and decide

44:03.130 --> 44:07.790
on the appropriate program for
all of those large institutions.

44:07.790 --> 44:12.320
We've strengthened the process
of supervision enormously

44:12.320 --> 44:19.450
since the crisis, and I feel a
very good sense of confidence

44:19.450 --> 44:21.580
in how we're carrying that out.

44:21.580 --> 44:27.880
Now, it is important to
make sure that we have fed

44:27.880 --> 44:35.310
into this process all the
information that's relevant

44:35.310 --> 44:37.700
to making the right decisions.

44:37.700 --> 44:42.940
And when there are
individuals who are examiners,

44:42.940 --> 44:47.250
who may disagree with others
in their team about how

44:47.250 --> 44:51.100
to interpret what's going on
at a particular institution,

44:51.100 --> 44:53.580
it's important that
there be channels

44:53.580 --> 44:58.340
by which they can make sure
that disagreements are fed

44:58.340 --> 45:00.880
up to the highest levels.

45:00.880 --> 45:03.590
This is true throughout
the work we do.

45:03.590 --> 45:06.390
We do economic forecasting,

45:06.390 --> 45:11.010
and our-the FOMC
receives information

45:11.010 --> 45:12.830
to help us make decisions,

45:12.830 --> 45:15.900
but obviously there are
disagreements about-among

45:15.900 --> 45:18.790
economists about how to
interpret developments.

45:18.790 --> 45:21.590
It's also important for us there

45:21.590 --> 45:25.340
to make sure we understand
alternative views.

45:25.340 --> 45:27.850
So this is important
in supervision.

45:27.850 --> 45:32.660
We've announced that the
Board has undertaken a review

45:32.660 --> 45:36.690
of whether or not there are
appropriate mechanisms in place

45:36.690 --> 45:40.960
in all of the Reserve Banks
that individuals who disagree

45:40.960 --> 45:46.700
with decisions can make their
own views known and feed

45:46.700 --> 45:50.490
into the process, and we've
also asked our inspector general

45:50.490 --> 45:53.580
to look into that.

45:53.580 --> 45:55.320
JEFF KEARNS.

45:57.710 --> 45:58.390
Thank you.

45:58.390 --> 46:00.170
Jeff Kearns from Bloomberg News.

46:00.170 --> 46:03.170
I'd also like to get off
monetary policy and ask you

46:03.170 --> 46:06.580
about the Federal Reserve's
relationship with Congress.

46:06.580 --> 46:10.960
Specifically, how worried
are you about legislation

46:10.960 --> 46:13.050
that has been proposed,
and may be proposed again

46:13.050 --> 46:18.060
in the next Congress, that
would reduce Fed independence?

46:18.060 --> 46:21.280
Would you see yourself
trying to fight back?

46:21.280 --> 46:23.550
Or would you see yourself
trying to go to Congress to work

46:23.550 --> 46:28.080
with them to do more with
transparency or something else

46:28.080 --> 46:35.890
to reduce their concerns
without making them law?

46:35.890 --> 46:40.310
And, if there were bills sent to
the White House, would you talk

46:40.310 --> 46:42.520
to the President
about vetoing them?

46:42.520 --> 46:46.360
Or do you have any confidence
that he would veto bills

46:46.360 --> 46:50.600
that would reduce the
Fed's independence?

46:50.600 --> 46:51.880
Thanks. CHAIR YELLEN.

46:51.880 --> 46:53.800
So let me simply say

46:53.800 --> 46:57.170
that Congress has
assigned us important tasks

46:57.170 --> 47:01.410
in monetary policy and in
other roles that we perform,

47:01.410 --> 47:06.400
and the Federal Reserve is
highly focused on attempting

47:06.400 --> 47:10.860
to carry out the mandates
that Congress has given us

47:10.860 --> 47:13.400
in the area of monetary policy.

47:13.400 --> 47:16.780
It's our dual mandate to
promote maximum employment

47:16.780 --> 47:21.300
and price stability, and
that's what we're working on.

47:21.300 --> 47:27.580
You know, I would say that
the ability of a central bank

47:27.580 --> 47:32.560
to make the decisions about
monetary policy that it regards

47:32.560 --> 47:36.620
as in the best longer-run
interests of the economy,

47:36.620 --> 47:41.550
free of short-run political
interference, is very important

47:41.550 --> 47:45.340
to the effective conduct
of monetary policy.

47:45.340 --> 47:50.170
And I think that history shows,
not only in the United States

47:50.170 --> 47:51.380
but around the world,

47:51.380 --> 47:56.250
that central bank
independence promotes better

47:56.250 --> 47:58.110
economic performance.

47:58.110 --> 48:02.740
So I do think central bank
independence is very important,

48:02.740 --> 48:07.710
and that it's important
to make sure

48:07.710 --> 48:12.000
that we can make the
decisions we think are best,

48:12.000 --> 48:15.190
free of short-run
political interference

48:15.190 --> 48:18.660
with respect to monetary policy.

48:18.660 --> 48:23.220
We should be accountable, and
we are accountable to Congress

48:23.220 --> 48:25.420
in explaining what we do.

48:25.420 --> 48:28.310
I believe strongly
in transparency,

48:28.310 --> 48:31.950
and I believe strongly
that we should communicate

48:31.950 --> 48:36.210
as clearly what we are doing
and the rationale for doing it,

48:36.210 --> 48:40.890
and I am very open to
looking for ways ourselves

48:40.890 --> 48:44.100
to improve our communications
and transparency,

48:44.100 --> 48:48.010
and working with
Congress to do that.

48:48.010 --> 48:53.690
But I would be very
concerned about actions.

48:53.690 --> 49:01.140
Back in 1978, Congress
explicitly passed legislation

49:01.140 --> 49:05.590
to ensure that there
would be no GAO audits

49:05.590 --> 49:08.560
of monetary policy
decisionmaking-

49:08.560 --> 49:10.430
namely, policy audits.

49:10.430 --> 49:14.960
I certainly hope that will
continue, and I will try

49:14.960 --> 49:18.350
to forcefully make the case
for why that's important.

49:18.350 --> 49:19.050
JEFF KEARNS.

49:19.050 --> 49:20.470
Would it veto?

49:20.470 --> 49:20.820
CHAIR YELLEN.

49:20.820 --> 49:22.280
I cannot speak for
the White House.

49:22.280 --> 49:24.150
I wouldn't attempt to do that.

49:24.150 --> 49:26.450
PETER BARNES.

49:26.450 --> 49:29.840
Peter Barnes of Fox
Business, ma'am.

49:29.840 --> 49:31.830
And also, I'll stay
off of interest rates.

49:31.830 --> 49:34.120
And first, I want to
wish you happy holidays.

49:34.120 --> 49:34.360
CHAIR YELLEN.

49:34.360 --> 49:34.600
Thank you.

49:34.600 --> 49:35.040
PETER BARNES.

49:35.040 --> 49:38.220
And, second, I want to ask
you about the Russian economy.

49:38.220 --> 49:40.660
Did that come up in the
meeting in your discussion

49:40.660 --> 49:42.880
about the global
economic developments?

49:42.880 --> 49:44.830
As you know, there's a lot
of concern that with the drop

49:44.830 --> 49:47.360
in oil prices, the
Russian economy could be

49:47.360 --> 49:48.330
in some trouble.

49:48.330 --> 49:52.740
Russia owes a lot of money
to U.S. and foreign banks

49:52.740 --> 49:54.530
and Russian companies.

49:54.530 --> 49:56.550
Is there any concern
about default?

49:56.550 --> 50:00.220
Any concern about
possible contagion?

50:00.220 --> 50:04.240
And, if so, has the Fed taken
any steps to prepare for that?

50:04.240 --> 50:04.750
Thank you.

50:04.750 --> 50:05.750
CHAIR YELLEN.

50:05.750 --> 50:12.330
Well, we certainly did review
global economic developments,

50:12.330 --> 50:17.110
including developments
in the Russian economy.

50:17.110 --> 50:21.470
Clearly, Russia has been
hit very hard by the decline

50:21.470 --> 50:29.010
in oil prices, and the ruble has
depreciated enormously in value,

50:29.010 --> 50:32.990
and this is posing a series

50:32.990 --> 50:38.230
of very difficult
economic conditions

50:38.230 --> 50:40.420
in the Russian economy.

50:40.420 --> 50:46.430
Of course we discussed what
the potential spillovers are

50:46.430 --> 50:51.040
to the United States, which
could occur both through trade

50:51.040 --> 50:53.440
and financial linkages.

50:53.440 --> 50:57.870
But these linkages are
actually relatively small.

50:57.870 --> 51:02.810
Russia accounts for less than
1 percent of U.S. trade volume,

51:02.810 --> 51:05.320
and U.S. banks' exposure

51:05.320 --> 51:10.130
to Russian residents
is really quite small

51:10.130 --> 51:14.530
in terms of-relative
to their capital.

51:14.530 --> 51:19.210
In terms of the portfolios
of U.S. residents,

51:19.210 --> 51:23.210
there are Russian securities,
but they are-they account

51:23.210 --> 51:25.980
for a very small share.

51:25.980 --> 51:32.030
So I expect that the linkages
back to the spillovers

51:32.030 --> 51:34.640
to the United States,
both through trade

51:34.640 --> 51:38.280
and financial channels,
would be small.

51:38.280 --> 51:41.950
Europe, of course, is somewhat
more exposed to Russia,

51:41.950 --> 51:46.310
both because Russia is an
important supplier of oil

51:46.310 --> 51:48.810
and natural gas to Europe

51:48.810 --> 51:52.350
and the financial linkages
are somewhat greater.

51:52.350 --> 51:55.060
But, in the case of
the United States,

51:55.060 --> 51:59.810
I see the spillovers
relative-it is pretty small.

51:59.810 --> 52:03.070
But we're obviously
watching that closely.

52:03.070 --> 52:06.100
GREG ROBB.

52:06.100 --> 52:13.530
Greg Robb from MarketWatch.

52:13.530 --> 52:14.540
Also, happy holidays.

52:14.540 --> 52:14.950
CHAIR YELLEN.

52:14.950 --> 52:15.430
Thank you.

52:15.430 --> 52:15.920
Same to you.

52:15.920 --> 52:17.440
GREG ROBB.

52:17.440 --> 52:21.540
There's a contagion risk
to the-from low oil prices

52:21.540 --> 52:23.900
that people are talking
about in the markets.

52:23.900 --> 52:27.010
What does it mean to the banks
that have lent, you know,

52:27.010 --> 52:30.350
into the oil patch with
the low oil prices?

52:30.350 --> 52:35.360
And I guess, you
know, your warnings

52:35.360 --> 52:38.040
about leveraged loans-you have
made warnings over the past year

52:38.040 --> 52:39.670
about leveraged lending.

52:39.670 --> 52:42.610
Are you worried that
they haven't been heeded?

52:42.610 --> 52:44.250
Thank you.

52:44.250 --> 52:47.320
CHAIR YELLEN.

52:47.320 --> 52:51.330
So, I mean, there is
some-you're talking

52:51.330 --> 52:53.940
about in the United
States exposure?

52:53.940 --> 52:59.880
I mean, we have seen some
impacts of lower oil prices

52:59.880 --> 53:07.110
on the spreads for high-yield
bonds, where there's exposure

53:07.110 --> 53:14.290
to oil companies that may
see distress or a decline

53:14.290 --> 53:19.660
in their earnings, and we have
seen some increase in spreads

53:19.660 --> 53:23.660
on high-yield bonds
more generally.

53:23.660 --> 53:30.820
I think for the banking
system, as a whole, the exposure

53:30.820 --> 53:38.080
to oil-I'm not aware of
significant issues there.

53:38.080 --> 53:42.730
This is the kind of thing that
is part of risk management

53:42.730 --> 53:46.110
for banking organizations
and the kind

53:46.110 --> 53:49.550
of thing they look
at in stress tests.

53:49.550 --> 53:53.840
But the movements in oil
prices have been very large

53:53.840 --> 53:56.580
and undoubtedly unexpected.

53:56.580 --> 54:01.870
We-in terms of leverage,
and whether

54:01.870 --> 54:07.450
or not levered entities could
be badly affected by movements

54:07.450 --> 54:13.660
in oil prices, leverage in the
financial system, in general,

54:13.660 --> 54:17.780
is way down from the
levels before the crisis.

54:17.780 --> 54:24.330
So it's not a major concern
that there are levered entities

54:24.330 --> 54:27.600
that would be badly
affected by this,

54:27.600 --> 54:31.410
but we'll have to
watch carefully.

54:31.410 --> 54:32.500
There have been large

54:32.500 --> 54:38.570
and unexpected movements
in oil prices.

54:41.830 --> 54:49.270
STEVE BECKNER.

54:49.270 --> 54:50.620
Good afternoon, Chair Yellen.

54:50.620 --> 54:52.300
Steve Beckner of MNI.

54:52.300 --> 54:55.420
I will go back to interest
rates, if you don't mind.

54:55.420 --> 54:57.920
Actually, it's a question
about balance sheet effects

54:57.920 --> 55:02.300
on the overall appropriate
level of monetary policy.

55:02.300 --> 55:06.410
In reaffirming the reinvestment
policy, the FOMC says,

55:06.410 --> 55:11.380
once again, that this will
help maintain accommodative

55:11.380 --> 55:12.990
financial conditions.

55:12.990 --> 55:15.660
In the past, it's said
that the large portfolio

55:15.660 --> 55:18.290
of securities will
exert a downward effect

55:18.290 --> 55:20.410
on long-term interest rates.

55:20.410 --> 55:23.870
As you look forward to
raising short-term rates,

55:23.870 --> 55:26.610
to what extent does
the FOMC need to take

55:26.610 --> 55:31.320
into account this sort of
residual, accommodative effect

55:31.320 --> 55:34.150
of maintaining a
large balance sheet?

55:34.150 --> 55:35.040
CHAIR YELLEN.

55:35.040 --> 55:41.180
So I agree, and that's why we
stated that we typically think

55:41.180 --> 55:49.160
of the monetary policy impact of
our asset purchases as depending

55:49.160 --> 55:54.630
on the stock of assets that we
hold on our balance sheet rather

55:54.630 --> 55:59.170
than the flow of purchases, and
so we're reminding the public

55:59.170 --> 56:03.580
that we continue to hold
a large stock of assets,

56:03.580 --> 56:08.890
and that is tending to
push down term premiums

56:08.890 --> 56:10.870
in longer-term yields.

56:10.870 --> 56:13.220
We made clear when we-or tried

56:13.220 --> 56:18.100
to make clear-when we issued
our normalization principles

56:18.100 --> 56:24.310
in September that we intend
to use changes in our target

56:24.310 --> 56:28.700
for the federal funds
rate as the main tool

56:28.700 --> 56:33.820
that we will actively use to
adjust financial conditions.

56:33.820 --> 56:42.010
Rather than actively planning to
sell the assets that we've put

56:42.010 --> 56:44.060
onto our balance sheet, sometime

56:44.060 --> 56:47.590
after we begin raising
our targets

56:47.590 --> 56:52.110
for short-term interest
rates, depending on economic

56:52.110 --> 56:56.310
and financial conditions,
we're likely to reduce

56:56.310 --> 56:59.660
or cease reinvestment
and gradually run

56:59.660 --> 57:01.920
down the stock of our assets.

57:01.920 --> 57:06.460
But our active tool for
adjusting monetary-the stance

57:06.460 --> 57:10.250
of monetary policy so
that it is appropriate

57:10.250 --> 57:15.810
for the economic needs for
the country, that will be done

57:15.810 --> 57:18.860
through adjusting our
short-term target range

57:18.860 --> 57:21.670
for the federal funds rate.

57:21.670 --> 57:28.490
KEVIN HALL.

57:28.490 --> 57:31.840
Kevin Hall with McClatchy
Newspapers.

57:31.840 --> 57:34.540
I can't believe nobody's asked
you the most important question,

57:34.540 --> 57:37.490
about what's going on with
your San Francisco 49ers,

57:37.490 --> 57:41.060
since everybody's already
wished you a happy holiday.

57:41.060 --> 57:43.050
Can you talk a little
bit about housing?

57:43.050 --> 57:45.880
Few things are more
important to Americans

57:45.880 --> 57:47.610
in their wealth creation
than housing.

57:47.610 --> 57:51.360
You've, in your statement, noted
that it continues to be a drag.

57:51.360 --> 57:54.730
Mr. Dudley has-was
actually relatively upbeat

57:54.730 --> 57:55.680
in his forecast.

57:55.680 --> 57:57.400
I don't know if that's a
view shared on the Committee.

57:57.400 --> 57:59.660
What do you think is
holding housing back?

57:59.660 --> 58:00.700
What can Congress do?

58:00.700 --> 58:02.980
What will you tell
Congress in the coming year?

58:02.980 --> 58:05.120
And more-and a clarification

58:05.120 --> 58:06.580
on the Dudley question
from earlier.

58:06.580 --> 58:09.220
You didn't mention him by
name in your being pleased

58:09.220 --> 58:10.870
by quality of supervision.

58:10.870 --> 58:14.100
Are you pleased with Mr.
Dudley's handling of the events?

58:14.100 --> 58:15.060
CHAIR YELLEN.

58:15.060 --> 58:16.450
So let me start with that.

58:16.450 --> 58:19.590
I have great confidence
in President Dudley.

58:19.590 --> 58:23.810
He's done a fine job in
running the New York Fed,

58:23.810 --> 58:29.700
and I want to be very clear that
I have great confidence in him.

58:29.700 --> 58:32.870
He's a distinguished
public servant,

58:32.870 --> 58:38.080
and he has worked very hard
in the aftermath of the crisis

58:38.080 --> 58:42.410
to make sure that the New York
Fed is doing all that it needs

58:42.410 --> 58:46.790
to do to contribute to
the work that we do both

58:46.790 --> 58:50.250
in financial stability
and in supervision.

58:50.250 --> 58:54.200
And let's see, the
other question

58:54.200 --> 58:55.930
that you asked was
about- KEVIN HALL.

58:55.930 --> 58:56.540
Housing. CHAIR YELLEN.

58:56.540 --> 58:58.020
-about housing.

58:58.020 --> 59:01.170
So, you know, I've
been surprised

59:01.170 --> 59:05.710
that housing hasn't recovered
more robustly than it has.

59:05.710 --> 59:11.340
In part, I think it reflects
very tight credit-continuing

59:11.340 --> 59:16.350
tight credit conditions
for any borrower

59:16.350 --> 59:21.770
that doesn't have really
pristine credit, you know,

59:21.770 --> 59:26.010
credit ratings, and my hope is

59:26.010 --> 59:29.980
that that situation
will ease over time.

59:29.980 --> 59:34.450
In addition, household formation
has been very depressed,

59:34.450 --> 59:39.280
and my expectation is that,
as the labor market continues

59:39.280 --> 59:46.590
to improve and households
feel better

59:46.590 --> 59:49.500
about their financial condition,

59:49.500 --> 59:53.090
that we will see
household formation pick up

59:53.090 --> 59:55.380
and a somewhat stronger recovery

59:55.380 --> 01:00:07.920
than we've seen thus
far in housing.

